#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""kgate: run a heavy gate on the homelab Kubernetes cluster instead of this Mac. Full doc: docs/kgate.md.

    kgate [--profile FILE] [--wait SECONDS] [--cpu N] [--mem SIZE] [--mem-limit SIZE] [--image REF] [--node NODE] [--sched-wait SECONDS] -- CMD [ARGS...]

heavy-gate's command line (`--wait SECONDS -- CMD`) plus the profile and a few overrides. Run it under
`run-and-wake -- kgate -- CMD` and the pane is woken when the job ends.

    kgate cancel [--wait SECONDS] JOB_ID      stop that one job (kgate's id or the run-and-wake id; exact match only)
    kgate cancel --list                       the jobs that are running now
    kgate cluster-setup NAME --from-context CTX   write a one-context kubeconfig and a cluster-file skeleton (once, for a profile with cluster = "NAME")
    kgate report [--days N] [--repo NAME] [--all] [--json]   read-only: per repo and command, the job log's runs, times, cores, memory and jobs that did not run
    kgate prune [--delete]                    retention for <state>/kgate-<epoch>-<pid>.out/: a dry run unless --delete (docs/kgate.md, "Prune")

`cancel` is the only supported way to stop a job: it signals that job's one client and nothing else. Never pkill.

Contract
- Ships the git worktree of $PWD (tracked and untracked, non-ignored files, its .git file and the shared git
  folder) to one pod, unpacked at the same absolute paths, and runs CMD in the same $PWD. Ignored files stay here.
  (On a kubectl cluster no .git is shipped: the pod builds a one-commit repo from the files, and the Mac's HEAD is in $KGATE_HEAD.)
- Tree hash: the git tree hash of the shipped files is computed here and again in the pod after unpacking. A
  difference fails the job before CMD runs.
- Profile (kgate.toml at the worktree root, or --profile FILE): image (pinned by digest), cpu (homelab: rounded up to whole 4-CPU slots, 1-3 -> 4, 6 -> 8; the client says so when it does), memory (the request),
  memory_limit (optional: the limit, default equal to memory), ephemeral_storage (the disk request, default 4Gi; ephemeral_storage_limit optional, default 12Gi or the request if larger), cpu_limit (optional, at least cpu; the pod's CPU limit, default none),
  workers_per_cpu (default 1: VITEST_MAX_WORKERS and CI_PAR_JOBS are the pod's CPUs times this), scratch_tmpfs (optional size, e.g. 256Mi: a memory-backed folder, TMPDIR points at it; counts toward the memory limit),
  tmp_tmpfs (optional size, e.g. 2Gi: /tmp itself is memory-backed, for a suite that names /tmp in code; counts toward the memory limit),
  priority_class (homelab, optional: kgate, kgate-first or kgate-main; env KGATE_PRIORITY_CLASS overrides it), the exact tool
  versions the gate needs, 1Password references for its secrets. The pod checks the versions before CMD; a mismatch
  fails the job with the tool, expected and found.
  services = ["docker"] (homelab, opt-in) with a [docker] table (image pinned by digest, cpu, cpu_limit, memory, memory_limit,
  ready_s: an integer of at least 30): the pod gets a privileged docker-in-docker native sidecar (emptyDir data root, no hostPath,
  no host socket; every container has a disk request and limit, and the data root's sizeLimit is the job's ephemeral_storage_limit, default 24Gi/40Gi with docker) and the job container, which stays unprivileged and keeps /cache, gets DOCKER_HOST. The pod runs in the
  `kgate-docker` PriorityClass and counts against the `kgate-docker` quota; priority_class together with docker is refused.
  A sidecar that does not become ready is exit 69 with its log tail and CMD not run. A sidecar that dies after CMD started
  leaves CMD's exit code alone, prints a note and records `docker_sidecar`; `docker_peak` is its peak memory. Docs: "Docker jobs".
- Exit status: CMD's own exit code when CMD ran to its end. 64 for a bad command line, or a CMD element that names a local file outside the worktree (the pod would not have it; nothing is submitted). 69 when the job could not
  start or finish on the cluster: unreachable, bad or missing profile, secret unreadable, pod never started, tree hash
  differs, tool version differs, the connection to a started pod was lost. 75 when the pod was still Unschedulable (PodScheduled=False)
  after --sched-wait SECONDS (default 1800, env KGATE_SCHED_WAIT; homelab only): the pod is deleted and CMD never ran. An evicted homelab pod is 69 with the reason `evicted: <the kubelet's message>`, read from the pod's status before it is deleted. kgate NEVER runs CMD on this Mac and never
  reruns a job that started, except once on a kubectl cluster when the cluster evicted the pod (confirmed from the pod's status or the events). A CMD that itself exits 69 looks the same by code; the record's `reason` tells them apart.
  When the homelab has no room, or its namespace's ResourceQuota refuses the pod, a job waits in a local queue (`kgate: queued: ...`, counted in --wait) instead of making a pod that would sit Unschedulable until 75; a lost pod's reason ends with `pod end reason: ...`. See docs/kgate.md.
{{ if eq .profile "personal" -}}
- 1Password reads (profile secrets and the pull token) call `op` by name. When `op` is the op shim and a Connect token is set, the shim sends them to the homelab Connect server; otherwise they use the service-account token: OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN, else
  ~/.config/agent-harness/op-service-token. Without a service-account token the job exits 69 before `op` runs, whatever the Connect token; `op` never reaches the desktop app.
{{ else -}}
- 1Password reads (profile secrets and the pull token) use the service-account token only: OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN, else
  ~/.config/agent-harness/op-service-token. With neither, the job exits 69 before `op` runs; `op` never reaches the desktop app.
{{ end }}  Each reference is read at most once in 24 hours: the value is cached in ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/kgate/ (see docs/kgate.md, "Secret cache").
- The node cache (/cache in the pod, /var/lib/kgate/cache on the node) is bounded: a job that starts on an idle node removes the least recently used cache entries past 8 GiB, and a running job's entries are never removed. Docs: "The node cache".
- Files CMD writes to /out in the pod come back to <state>/<id>.out/ before the pod is deleted.
- The pod is deleted on every path (CMD's exit, 69, an interrupt). A failed delete is retried for up to 60 s; the record's `cleanup` says whether it was confirmed and `cleanup_attempts` how many tries it took.
- A job whose client has died is ended by the next kgate job that starts on the same cluster (exit 143, `reason` naming the gone client, pod deleted), before that job creates its own pod. Every pod also ends itself at 4 hours on the homelab (the cluster file's `deadline_s` on another cluster).
- One JSON line per job in ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/kgate/log.jsonl (keyed by the run-and-wake job id when
  there is one); the full output is in <id>.log beside it.

A second cluster (cluster = "NAME" in the profile): the same contract on a Kubernetes cluster reached through local kubectl. Everything about the
cluster is in ~/.config/kgate/clusters/NAME.toml, not in this file (`kgate cluster-setup` writes a skeleton). No Secrets, no python3 in the pod: the
pod side is a POSIX sh script that prints the same markers. See "A second cluster" in docs/kgate.md.

Env: KGATE_SSH (default: ~/.config/kgate/host, rendered from the chezmoi data keys sshHosts.kgate and the optional sshHosts.kgateFallback), KGATE_SSH_KEY (default ~/.ssh/homelab).
Either one is a single user@address or an ordered list (commas or lines). At job start kgate uses the first host that answers ssh and says which.
The same file runs in the pod as `kgate.py --pod JOB.json`: it verifies the tree hash, checks the tool versions,
runs CMD and reports peak memory. Needs python3 3.11+ in the image.
"""
